#{[17592186335627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335627 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "daa4074b-77d0-50c6-a0c2-ed91e9092a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335635 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e443c3bb-f95f-5b04-a2c2-a7d665f716b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335941 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the angle between $a=⟨-1,7,0⟩$ and $b=⟨3,6,-1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa6fe310-eeef-5a18-a61a-06fedd1010e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335941 #edb/ref 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 3+7\\cdot 6+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{\\sqrt{50}\\cdot \\sqrt{46}} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{\\sqrt{2300}} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{10\\sqrt{23}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{39}{10\\sqrt{23}})≈{35.6}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336007 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $∠PQR$ given the points $P(-3,0,-1)$, $Q(-1,1,0)$, and $R(1,2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06345d34-3e03-5708-b180-d74d0f414ffb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336007 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨-3,0,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨-1,1,0⟩,\\,r=⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠PQR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/20/209d43a5aab5d57fbd4cd7b421d41f0fa85bc58cfdd36251a8acf392b0ba7312.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{QP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{QR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QP} & =p-q \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,-1⟩-⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{QR} & =r-q \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩-⟨-1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{QP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{QR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{QP}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{QR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{QP}||\\overset{\\to }{QR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336010 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336010 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336010 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $∠XZY$ given the points $X(-1,0,3)$, $Y(-2,2,3)$, and $Z(-2,-1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1615350f-44ad-5e4b-849c-6c410f4c58d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336010 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,y=⟨-2,2,3⟩,\\,z=⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/87/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342251 #edb/ref 1080 "{58}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342251 #edb/